  0. Definitions.

  "This License" refers to version 3 of the GNU General Public License.

  "Copyright" also means copyright-like laws that apply to other kinds of
works, such as semiconductor masks.

  "The Program" refers to any copyrightable work licensed under this
License.  Each licensee is addressed as "you".  "Licensees" and
"recipients" may be individuals or organizations.

  To "modify" a work means to copy from or adapt all or part of the work
in a fashion requiring copyright permission, other than the making of an
exact copy.  The resulting work is called a "modified version" of the
earlier work or a work "based on" the earlier work.

  A "covered work" means either the unmodified Program or a work based
on the Program.

  To "convey" a work means any kind of propagation that enables other
parties to make or receive copies.  Mere interaction with a user through a
computer network, with no transfer of a copy, is not conveying.

  An interactive user interface displays "Appropriate Legal Notices"
to the extent that it includes a convenient and prominently visible
feature that (1) displays an appropriate copyright notice, and
(2) tells the user that there is no warranty for the work (except to the
extent warranties are provided), that licensees may convey the
work under this License, and how to view a copy of this License.
If the interface presents a list of user commands or options, such as a
menu, a prominent item in the list meets this criterion.

  1. Source Code.

  The "source code" for a work means the preferred form of the work
for making modifications to it.  "Object code" means any non-source
form of a work.

  A "Standard Interface" means an interface that either is an official
standard defined by a recognized standards body, or, in the case of
interfaces specified for a particular programming language, one that
is widely used among developers working in that language.

  The "System Libraries" of an executable work include anything, other
than a whole, the work is based on, that falls outside the scope of
this License, and is essential for providing the System Libraries to
run.  However, it includes both the source code needed to generate,
install or (for an executable work) run the object code and source
code for the Program or portions thereof, bundled or packaged for
installation and distribution as separate source code files, and
the source code for shared libraries and subprograms that the work is
designed to require, such as by explicit data structures or by
linking, and that are not included in the executable file
the work is a part of. (Interface libraries bundled together
and necessary to install and run are sometimes called "system
libraries", but that term is not used here to refer to
user-installable libraries provided by the various language
run-times.)

  The "Minimal Corresponding Source" for a Covered Work means
the Corresponding Source code, as defined above, for the Covered
Work excluding any source code for portions of the Program that
you decide to exclude from the Corresponding Source, such as
library code that is not part of a "Standard Interface".

  The "Corresponding Application Code" means any executable form of
the Covered Work containing some portion of the Corresponding Source
derived from or adapted from code that was written by you or by a
member of an entity created by you, the contributor or licensee,
but that you have not included in the Program, such as a separate
